---
export const prerender = true;
import Layout from '../../layouts/main.astro';
import CaseHeader from '../../components/CaseHeader.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import inbox from '../../assets/projects/dispatch-inbox.png';
import reader from '../../assets/projects/dispatch-reader.png';
---

<Layout
  title="Dispatch"
  description="A native SwiftUI email application developed from Windows, using XcodeGen and macOS GitHub Actions for builds and simulator tests."
>
  <article class="wrap page">
    <CaseHeader
      title="Dispatch"
      description="Native iOS email, developed from Windows."
      stack="SwiftUI / SwiftData / Gmail API / GitHub Actions"
    >
      <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/pphilfre/mail">
        Source ↗
      </a>
      <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/pphilfre/mail/releases">
        IPA releases ↗
      </a>
    </CaseHeader>
    <figure class="case-image">
      <div class="case-phone-gallery">
        <a
          class="case-image-link"
          href={inbox.src}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
          aria-label="Open Dispatch inbox screenshot at full size (new tab)"
        >
          <Image
            loading="lazy"
            src={inbox}
            alt="Dispatch inbox showing sample messages"
            widths={[360, 600, 900]}
            sizes="(max-width: 700px) 38vw, 280px"
            format="webp"
            quality={95}
          />
        </a>
        <a
          class="case-image-link"
          href={reader.src}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
          aria-label="Open Dispatch reader screenshot at full size (new tab)"
        >
          <Image
            loading="lazy"
            src={reader}
            alt="Dispatch conversation reader showing sample messages"
            widths={[360, 600, 900]}
            sizes="(max-width: 700px) 38vw, 280px"
            format="webp"
            quality={95}
          />
        </a>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        Actual simulator captures of Dispatch, using sample messages. Select
        either image to enlarge.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>A native mail app</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          I wanted to explore a native email app with familiar iOS controls and
          more ways to organise mail. My development computer runs Windows, so
          the project also became an exercise in making an Apple application
          maintainable without local Xcode.
        </p>
        <p>
          Dispatch uses SwiftUI for the interface and Gmail for connected
          accounts. My work includes planning the behaviour, configuring builds,
          reviewing test results and improving the app through testing,
          alongside implementation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>From Windows to iOS</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          I work with Swift source files and an XcodeGen specification in{' '}
          <code>project.yml</code>. GitHub Actions runs on macOS, generates the
          Xcode project, compiles the app and runs unit and UI tests in an
          iPhone simulator. Build logs and test result bundles make failures
          inspectable from Windows.
        </p>
        <p>
          The release workflow runs full simulator validation alongside the
          device build. Publication waits for both jobs, then attaches an IPA
          and checksum to a GitHub release. The IPA is unsigned and needs
          re-signing with a compatible sideloading tool before installation.
        </p>
        <p>
          Keeping the project definition in text avoids maintaining a generated
          Xcode project by hand. It also makes configuration changes reviewable
          alongside the Swift changes they support.
        </p>
        <a
          class="text-link"
          href="https://github.com/pphilfre/mail/blob/main/.github/workflows/release.yml"
        >
          Release workflow ↗
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>Mail & local storage</h2>
      <div class="decisions">
        <div>
          <h3>Implemented</h3>
          <p>
            Gmail OAuth, cached conversations, incremental synchronisation,
            search, read/star/archive/trash actions, local drafts, sending,
            replies and attachments are implemented. The interface reads cached
            mail first, so displaying an existing message does not depend on
            waiting for a fresh network response.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Credentials and persistence</h3>
          <p>
            OAuth uses a native authentication session with PKCE. Access and
            refresh tokens belong in device-local Keychain storage. SwiftData
            holds accounts, cached messages, drafts and pending operations;
            Gmail credentials do not enter that database.
          </p>
          <p>
            The storage code preserves the original data if importing older
            drafts fails. An unavailable database produces a recovery state
            instead of silently opening an empty replacement.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>When syncing went wrong</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          Loading older mail exposed Gmail throttling and a sync control that
          stayed busy through retries. The investigation separated request
          scheduling from the visible loading state, rather than treating every
          failure as a permissions problem.
        </p>
        <p>
          The provider now shares request pacing and server cooldowns across
          each account, honours <code>Retry-After</code> and reuses cached
          message bodies on retries. Regression tests cover partial-page
          downloads and releasing the busy state after failures.
        </p>
        <p>
          Sending has a different risk: a lost response does not prove an email
          was not sent. Uncertain sends are kept for review and checked against
          Sent, rather than automatically sent again.
        </p>
        <a
          class="text-link"
          href="https://github.com/pphilfre/mail/blob/main/docs/gmail-sync-investigation.md"
        >
          Read the sync investigation ↗
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>Validation & next steps</h2>
      <div>
        <p>
          Simulator fixtures exercise app behaviour without sending personal
          email. They do not establish that live Google login, delivery or
          installation works on a particular signed device. The latest UI and
          writing-assistance work also has further device checks documented in
          the repository.
        </p>
        <p>
          Zoho support and APNs push delivery remain planned. Background refresh
          code exists in newer work, but its provisioning and device behaviour
          still need validation; I do not treat it as guaranteed background
          delivery.
        </p>
        <p>
          This project has helped me understand the difference between compiling
          an app, testing its behaviour and validating it on a device. It has
          also made persistence, retry handling and clear failure states feel
          much more concrete.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <a class="next-project" href="/projects/waypoint">
      <span>Next project</span>
      <strong>
        Waypoint <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </strong>
    </a>
  </article>
</Layout>
